{
  // Adeptsworn public website — https://adeptsworn.com
  //
  // THIS USED TO SAY "this is not a program". As of 8 September 2026 it
  // is one: there is a Worker script now, and it does exactly one job —
  // signing in with Discord. Everything else on this site is still a
  // plain file served without any code running.
  //
  // "name" MUST match the existing Cloudflare Worker exactly. Change it and
  //   wrangler builds a NEW Worker under the new name and leaves the domain
  //   pointing at the old one. It is an identity, not a label.
  // "compatibility_date" pins the Cloudflare runtime behaviour to how it
  //   worked on this date, so a future change on their end cannot silently
  //   alter how the site is served. Leave it alone unless you have a reason.
  // "assets.directory" is what gets published. "./" means this whole folder.
  // "main" is the Worker script. Cloudflare serves a matching FILE first and
  //   only calls the script when there is no file at that address — so
  //   index.html, assets\ and robots.txt reach people exactly as before,
  //   and a bug in the script cannot take the site down. Only /auth/... is
  //   handled by code.
  // "assets.binding" lets the script hand a request back to the file server.
  //   It is only there as a safety net for an address that is neither.
  //
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  WHY THE CLIENT ID IS IN THIS FILE AND THE OTHER TWO ARE NOT
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The Discord Client ID is PUBLIC BY DESIGN. It travels in the address
  // bar of every person who signs in, so putting it in a public repo
  // gives away nothing that was not already on show.
  //
  // Keeping it here rather than in the Cloudflare dashboard is deliberate.
  // Cloudflare's own documentation does NOT settle what happens to a
  // plain variable set in the dashboard when a later deploy arrives with
  // a config that does not mention it (checked 8 Sept, not remembered).
  // A value declared HERE cannot be quietly lost that way, and it is
  // version-controlled besides. So: sidestep the question rather than
  // find out the hard way, six weeks from now, with a login that stopped
  // working and nothing in a log to say why.
  //
  // THE OTHER TWO ARE REAL SECRETS AND MUST NEVER APPEAR IN THIS
  // REPOSITORY, in this file or any other:
  //
  //   DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET   set on the Worker in Cloudflare, encrypted
  //   SESSION_SECRET          same. Ours. Any long random string.
  //
  // Cloudflare's docs DO confirm secrets survive a deploy. A secret
  // committed to a public repo is burned the moment it lands, and
  // deleting it afterwards does not help — the commit stays in a public
  // history.
  //
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  ONE UNTIDINESS, WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN HIDDEN
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Because the assets directory is this whole folder, src\index.js is
  // ALSO published as a file, so https://adeptsworn.com/src/index.js can
  // be read by anyone. Cloudflare documents no way to exclude a file from
  // an assets folder — no .assetsignore, nothing in the assets config
  // (three of their doc pages checked on 8 Sept). It leaks nothing: this
  // repository is public, so that code was already readable on GitHub,
  // and no secret is in any file here. The tidy fix, if it ever irritates,
  // is to move the site's files into a public\ subfolder and point
  // "directory" at it — but that moves index.html too, and
  // Publish-Website.bat finds this folder BY index.html. Change both or
  // neither.

  "name": "wispy-brook-7be4",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-06",
  "main": "./src/index.js",
  "vars": {
    "DISCORD_CLIENT_ID": "1546109338169639013"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  }
}
